pwd  : directory you presently are working on
ls: list content of current working directory
cd : change directory
clear : to clear the terminal

Navigation :=====
cd .. or go deeper with cd ../..
ls .. 

create and Delete Files :=====
touch or new-file-items(windows) filename.txt
rm filename.txt

create and delete directories :=====
mkdir dir_name
rmdir dir_name
rm -r dir_name  (use this when dir is not empty)


write to files :======
echo 'content to write'  (this just print to terminal)
echo 'content to write' > hello.txt ( this overrite existing content)
echo 'content to write' >> hello.txt 

Read Files :======
cat filename.txt (prints ouptut to stdout i.e terminal)
cat hello.txt > hello_copy.txt ( read file content and writes into the other file it will create if file does not exist)
cat hello.txt >> hello_copy.txt (to append rather than overwrite)

echo $0: Tells you if you using shell or bash

Options :====
There are options thatcan be attached to a command to change its default behaviore.g
rm -r (r = recursive)
ls -l (l= long format)


find Files and Directories :======
 find [path] [option] [expression]
 e.g find . -name 'forest*'
 find options are:
    search by name: -name 'forest*'
    case insensitive search by name: -iname 'forest*'
    search byt: -type f (files) or -type d (directories)
    combine options: -type d -iname 'forest*'

find .. -type f -iname  'large*' ( mean find a file in above dir with wildcard name that starts with large)

Rename and moving of file :========
mv [old_name.ext] [new_name.ext] (renaming)
mv [name.txt] [new_location] (if path does not exist it will rename the file)
cp [original_name.txt] [copy_name.txt]  
cp -r cities cities_backup (add r for directories)


Search Files :==============
grep [patten] [files(s)]
grep 'CEO' team.txt (grep gives all instances of the pattern you looking for and when it finds that pattern, it prints its entire line)

grep [option] [pattern] [files(s)]
grep -n ',' team*
result: team.txt:1:AB, CEO and creative genius
result: team.txt:2: NN, hard working intern

-n -> means show line numbers
-i -> case insensitive
-r -> search recursively

Replace content in files :========
sed 's/pattern/replacement/' [filename]
e.g sed 's/,/:/' team*
note: note this only change first instance and also changes is only on the termninal, it does not affect the main file

sed 's/pattern/replacement/[option(s)]' [filename]
e.g .g sed 's/,/:/gI' team*
- the g makes it global not just first instance
- I means its case insensitive

we can change the file with 2 options
1. write to a new file
e.g sed 's/,/:/' team* > new-file.txt
2. Modify the file directly
e.g sed -i 's/,/:/' team*
on mac sed -i '' 's/,/:/' team*


Count values in files :======
wc [filenmae]
e.g wc team.txt
 5 17 112 team.txt
 5 number of lines
 17 no of words
 112 bytes
 there is always a first empty space at the begining

 options
 wc -l, -w, -c, -,
 using the option l -> no lines only
 -w -> no of word count only
 -c - no of characters only


 Sort files content :======
 sort [options] [filename]
 e.g sort team.txt
 options:  -r -> reverse Z-A, -n -> numeric runmbers
 uniq [filename] -> remove duplicates

 using pipe character to combine
 e.g sort [filename] | uniq

 store the output:
 e.g sort [filename] | uniq > [newfilename]
 